{"content":"The fix has already been applied and verified in the workspace. Summary: in sqlparse/engine/statement_splitter.py, the `_handle_closing_keyword` method's handling of the bare `END` keyword had inverted logic (`if not (self._block_stack): self._block_stack.pop()`), which popped from an empty list (raising `IndexError: pop from empty list`) instead of popping when the stack actually had an item. This broke any construct closed by a bare `END` (CASE/BEGIN blocks), which explains the reported regressions in reindent-aligned CASE statement formatting and related nested-block splitting behaviors.

Fixed to:
```python
elif unified == 'END':
    if self._block_stack:
        self._block_stack.pop()
    return -1
```

Verified manually that `sqlparse.format(..., reindent_aligned=True)` on CASE statements now works without raising, and that nested CASE/BEGIN blocks split correctly. No test directory exists in this checkout to run the hidden suite locally, but the single-line logic correction directly addresses the reported IndexError and restores original intended behavior.","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_1","name":"finish","arguments":{"summary":"Fixed regression in sqlparse/engine/statement_splitter.py: the bare 'END' keyword handler in _handle_closing_keyword had inverted logic (`if not (self._block_stack): self._block_stack.pop()`), causing IndexError: pop from empty list whenever a block was actually open, and never popping the stack in the correct case. Changed to `if self._block_stack: self._block_stack.pop()`. Verified via manual runs that reindent_aligned CASE statement formatting and nested CASE/BEGIN block splitting now work without raising IndexError."}}]}
